														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4651776">
									

<p>I largely agree with <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/alandaris" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3146689); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3146689&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1589758473" alt="AlanDaris" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3146689)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/alandaris" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3146689); return false;">AlanDaris</a></span>'s view that all of the potential horror that comes from a powerful organization utilizing their funds to extract the souls of children and place them inside of arcade machines are boiled down to cheap scares and artificially creepy children chanting in a monotone voice<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-733395-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-733395-1')">1</a></sup>. The final addendum is painfully cliché, with the child giving all three of the most mind-numbing descriptions of a potentially anomalous murderer imaginable: an angel, a dark man, and a liar. It simply kills all sense of uniqueness and charm that this article had left, which was what was keeping it afloat for me.</p>
<p>A smaller note, but I don't like the inclusion of the fading text on the first addendum. While it works in an article like <a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/scp-5656">SCP-5656</a>, which is a heavy format screw, it doesn't add anything here besides just shortening the one section where I could potentially empathize with the parents of these missing children. Thus, it again kills a lot of the potential emotional investment that I could've shared with these characters.</p>
<p>This is a -1 from me.</p>
